<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Zhidong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chang Chih-tung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Zhidong was a Chinese official of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> who advocated controlled reform and the modernisation of the army, serving as viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguang">Huguang</a> from 1889. He ordered an iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> plant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which began production in 1894 but made substantial losses, and founded a mint, mills and factories. His Exhortation to Study of 1898 summed up his relatively conservative reform in the phrase &quot;Chinese learning as substance, Western learning for application&quot;. He helped to lead the abolition of the imperial examination in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong.jpg" alt="Zhang Zhidong" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Zhidong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhang Zhidong was born on 2 September 1837 in Xingyi Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a>, though his family's roots lay in Nanpi in Zhili. He came first in the provincial examination of 1852 and in 1863 placed third in the palace examination, entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a> as an editor. In 1882 he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. During the Ili Crisis he demanded the execution of the envoy Chonghou, who had signed a treaty granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> extraterritorial rights and an indemnity, and urged the government to declare it invalid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> of 1885 Zhang called for a modern army. He founded the Guangdong Military Academy and the Guangdong Victorious Army with German officers as instructors, and in 1896 the Hubei Military Academy. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> he trained modern units of engineers, cavalry, artillery and infantry armed with Mauser rifles; of his 60,000 men, 20,000 were trained directly by foreign officers. He was put in charge of a railway from Hankou towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, finished in 1906.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> he urged foreign loans, arms purchases and alliances, and in 1895 he proposed borrowing from the British in return for their navy's defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> in 1900 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and other governors in the Mutual Protection of Southeast China, refusing to take part in the court's war against the Eight-Nation Alliance. His informant Yang Rui, one of the Six Martyrs, carried out his instructions during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> of 1898. Exhortation to Study proposed new schools funded from Buddhist and Taoist monasteries, of which 30 percent would be kept. In 1901 he succeeded Liu Kunyi as viceroy of Liangjiang at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, where he helped found Sanjiang Normal College, and in 1906 he became Minister of Military Affairs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zhang died of illness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 4 October 1909, aged 72, and received the posthumous name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenxiang">Wenxiang</a>. Historians commonly see his reforms as an attempt to reconcile modernity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s existing social fabric. In 1911 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> garrison he had built led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a>, which set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a>. Red Guards destroyed his tomb in 1966; his remains were found again in 2007 and reburied.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Zhidong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The balance between old and new was later summed up by <b>Zhang Zhidong</b>, in his Exhortation to Study of 1898, as “Chinese learning for the substance, Western learning for practical use”: Western knowledge was to serve Chinese values and institutions, not to replace them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nanpi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.04,116.7&amp;year=1883" aria-label="Nanpi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7457 796 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhang Zhidong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7907 1066h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7907 1066h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.04,116.7&amp;year=1883">What was here in 1883? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
